\details{
The decision time is \code{Wald(mu, boundary) + Exponential(1 / tau)}: evidence
accumulates at rate \code{mu} until it reaches \code{boundary}, and an exponentially
distributed residual stage of mean \code{tau} follows. It is the \strong{mechanistic
counterpart of \code{\link[=cogmod_exgaussian]{cogmod_exgaussian()}}}, whose first stage is a descriptive
Gaussian rather than a decision process, and \code{tau} means the same thing in
both.

\code{ndt} and \code{poutlier} mean exactly what they do in \code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}},
and \code{\link[=with_outliers]{with_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=without_outliers]{without_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=p_outlier]{p_outlier()}} and
\code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} all work here too. See \code{?rcogmod_lognormal} for why \code{ndt} is
expressed directly in seconds, what the outlier component is for, and why its
scale is a constant rather than a \code{dpar}.

The mean exists and is \code{ndt + boundary / mu + tau}, so \code{posterior_epred()}
returns a number - unlike \code{\link[=cogmod_invgaussian]{cogmod_invgaussian()}} once its drift varies.
}
\section{\code{ndt} and \code{tau}}{
Both delay the response, and they are separated only by shape: \code{ndt} is a
hard floor, \code{tau} a variable stage with an exponential spread. That makes
them a ridge rather than a flat direction - the leading edge of the
distribution identifies \code{ndt} - but a ridge all the same. The \code{normal(-1.2, 0.2)} that \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} puts on \code{ndt} is what holds it; widen it and
expect the two to trade off. Schwarz's own model has no \code{ndt} at all, the
exponential stage being the whole of the residual time, and fixing \code{ndt = 0}
in \code{bf()} recovers exactly that.

There is deliberately no \code{sigmadrift}. It and \code{tau} both fatten the right
tail and are very hard to tell apart; \code{\link[=cogmod_invgaussian]{cogmod_invgaussian()}} is where the
drift-variability route lives.
}

\section{How the density is computed}{
Worth knowing, because the two branches are not equally common. Writing
\code{g = 1 / tau}, the convolution has an elementary closed form,
\verb{g * exp(-g * t + boundary * (mu - k)) * F_Wald(t; k, boundary)} with
\code{k = sqrt(mu^2 - 2 * g)}, whenever \code{mu^2 > 2 / tau}. At a drift of 3 and a
threshold of 0.5 that asks for \verb{tau > 0.22 s}, where a residual stage is more
often nearer 0.1 s, so the other branch is reached routinely.

There \code{k} is imaginary, and the same expression continues analytically into
\code{g * exp(-(boundary - mu * t)^2 / (2 * t)) * Re[w(z)]}, where \code{w} is the
Faddeeva function and \code{z = (kappa * sqrt(t) + i * boundary / sqrt(t)) / sqrt(2)} with \code{kappa = sqrt(2 / tau - mu^2)}. The exponent is the Wald's own,
so nothing overflows, and \code{w} is evaluated by Weideman's 24-term rational
approximation. \strong{Both branches are exact}, and they meet exactly at
\code{mu^2 = 2 / tau} - at \code{kappa = 0} the second reduces to the first - so the
relative step measured either side of the seam is 5e-8, which is rounding.

Quadrature on the original convolution integral was tried first and does not
work: the log-integrand is bimodal, with one peak at the Wald bulk near zero
and another at \code{u = t} where \code{exp(u / tau)} is climbing, and their widths vary
independently over orders of magnitude. Fixed-panel rules reach only 1e-1
relative error somewhere in the region an RT fit actually visits, and leave a
step of 0.74 at the seam.
}

